#=====================================================================
# test/diag/tdggrd.ref
#
# Reference-value data for PROGRAM TDGGRD (test/diag/tdggrd.f).
#
# Routine under test : modern/diag/diaglog.f  (guard clause)
# Reference source   : bin/nastrn.f (LOUT=3); mis/mesage.f (MESAGE)
#
# DELIVERABLE -- used in Solaris; not executed by Blitzy.
# This file is reference DATA only.  It is NOT a *.f source and is NOT
# matched by the bin/linknas "*.f" build glob, so it is never compiled
# or linked.  It is read/inspected by the unit driver and the test
# harness in the developer's Solaris environment (csh + Sun f77).
#
# Expected verdict   : PASS: TDGGRD
#=====================================================================
#
# PURPOSE
#   TDGGRD verifies the GUARD-CLAUSE ZERO-OVERHEAD property of the
#   modern diagnostic layer: every routine in modern/diag/ begins
#   with the guard clause
#
#       IF (IDIAG .EQ. 0) RETURN
#
#   as its FIRST executable statement.  When diagnostics are disabled
#   (IDIAG = 0) the routine returns immediately and performs no I/O,
#   so it incurs no measurable cost.  The only portable behavioral
#   proof of this property is "disabled => zero output": a call to
#   modern/diag/diaglog.f while IDIAG = 0 must write ZERO records to
#   logical unit 3 (LOUT).  This reference encodes that expectation.
#
# RATIONALE (evidence from the AAP and the reference sources)
#   * The guard clause as the first executable statement is a HARD
#     success criterion of the refactor (AAP 0.6.4, 0.7.2, 0.7.3):
#     every diagnostic routine begins with IF (IDIAG .EQ. 0) RETURN
#     as its first executable statement, verifiable by inspection.
#   * IDIAG is the single diagnostic-enable state.  It is set once by
#     modern/diag/diagctl.f from the NASTRAN_* environment toggles;
#     when those toggles are unset IDIAG = 0, the layer is OFF, and
#     behavior is exactly APR.95 (default safety, AAP 0.7.4).
#   * bin/nastrn.f makes logical unit 3 the NASTRAN log: after the
#     bootstrap CALL DBMINT it sets LOUT = 3 and opens unit 3
#     (OPEN(3,FILE=DSNAMES(3),STATUS='UNKNOWN')).  Unit 3 is thus the
#     only sink a modern diagnostic write could reach.
#   * mis/mesage.f shows the LEGACY MESAGE writer targets NOUT
#     (COMMON /SYSTEM/ IBUF,NOUT,... ; WRITE(NOUT,...)), i.e. the
#     legacy print stream, NOT unit 3.  The guard short-circuits
#     before any write, so when disabled nothing is emitted to unit 3
#     and nothing is added to any other stream.
#
# BINDING EXPECTATION
#   With diagnostics DISABLED (IDIAG = 0), a call to DIAGLOG with any
#   in-band MESAGE code (9001-9999) writes EXACTLY ZERO records to
#   logical unit 3 (LOUT).  The guard clause IF (IDIAG .EQ. 0) RETURN
#   short-circuits the routine before any I/O is performed, on unit 3
#   or on any other unit.
#
# CONSISTENCY
#   DISABLED_IDIAG and EXPECT_UNIT3_RECORDS below MUST match the
#   constants asserted in test/diag/tdggrd.f (disabled => zero unit-3
#   records) and the guard-clause implementation in
#   modern/diag/diaglog.f (IF (IDIAG .EQ. 0) RETURN as the first
#   executable statement).  If the guard-clause contract ever changes,
#   update this reference and the driver together.
#
#---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Machine-readable key/value lines (whitespace separated, one per
# line).  These are the values the driver and harness parse.
#---------------------------------------------------------------------
DISABLED_IDIAG 0
EXPECT_UNIT3_RECORDS 0
EXPECT_VERDICT PASS: TDGGRD
